  - $agent-update-flow
decisions:
  - id: documentor-flag-only
    decision: Documentor does NOT write university content. It flags stale content only. University writing is gated by agent-prefs, not hardcoded.
    rationale: 'Jinx: Documentor runs at haiku (tier-3), educational content requires tier-2+. Haiku would produce .purpose rephrases disguised as notes — a knowledge pollution engine. Educator agent already exists at tier-2 with content design notebooks.'
  - id: adoption-not-recurring-config
    decision: Agent preferences are a one-time adoption ceremony (like hiring), not recurring configuration. EULA-style scope approval on version updates.
    rationale: 'User framing: ''just like hiring a new team member you want to make sure even you understand what they are doing.'' One-time at project start or agent install. Updates show scoped diffs for quick approval.'
  - id: scoped-permissions-model
    decision: Agents declare scopes (read, write, tool, net, exec) in .agent files. 5-6 coarse categories, not fine-grained. Auto-approve when scopes unchanged on version bumps.
    rationale: 'Jinx: fine-grained scopes become click-through fatigue within a month. Coarse scopes with batch approval and auto-approve for same-scope updates. Maps to OAuth model (Meta/Google ad networks).'
  - id: shift-guaranteed-files
    decision: paradigm shift guarantees 31 files/directories exist after completion. Creates skeletons for detected ecosystems only, not all possible agents.
    rationale: 'User: ''certain files are just not always added whenever a project is initialized.'' Jinx: create only for detected ecosystems, use post-shift recommendations for optional ones. Never truly blank — pre-fill with role defaults + project-type-specific patterns.'
  - id: post-shift-recommendations
    decision: Shift outputs conditional, actionable recommendations (max 3-4 items) based on actual project state, replacing the current hardcoded 'Next steps' text.
    rationale: 'Loid: conditional recommendations get read, static ones get ignored. Each item has a copy-paste command + one-line rationale. Mature projects show ''No recommendations — everything looks good.'''
  - id: adoptions-yaml
    decision: New .paradigm/adoptions.yaml unifies core roster and marketplace agent records. Tracks adoption date, source, defaults-accepted, overrides, and marketplace version/offerings.
    rationale: 'Apex: unifies two currently separate flows (roster for core, install for marketplace) into one adoption model. The adoption record is the single source of truth for what agents a project uses and what the user agreed to.'
  - id: pan-standard
    decision: Agent contracts and scoped permissions become PAN (Paradigm Agent Norm) — the standard for any agent including Neverland marketplace agents.
    rationale: 'User: ''this even feels like it is a PAN / project neverland agent system norm that should be standardized.'' All three agents agree marketplace agents need a common protocol for declaring capabilities and respecting project context.'
  - id: ship-both-phases-together
    decision: Contracts (transparency) and preferences (configuration) ship in one release, not phased.
    rationale: 'User: ''let''s go big and then from user feedback we will adjust accordingly.'' Jinx agrees: both features modify the same surfaces (.agent schema, shift.ts, roster). One disruption is better than two separate migrations.'
  - id: consider-identity-migration
    decision: Consider landing three-layer identity model (id/nickname/archetype) in this release to avoid future migration debt for Neverland.
    rationale: 'Jinx: ecosystem agents are a new archetype category. Shipping them on the current two-field identity (id+nickname) means another migration when nevr.land launches. Can accept the debt if scope is too large.'
learnings:
  - 'Three rounds of team analysis progressively refined the proposal: Round 1 debated Documentor extension (Jinx killed tier mismatch), Round 2 expanded to agent preferences (Jinx killed recurring config), Round 3 converged on adoption ceremonies with scoped permissions (all approved)'
  - 'The user''s hiring analogy resolved the ''configuration hell'' concern: one-time adoption is fundamentally different from recurring preference prompts. The team''s initial framing of ''preferences'' was wrong — the user was describing ''informed hiring decisions'''
  - Jinx's separation of transparency (understand what agents do) from configuration (change how they behave) was the key architectural insight — both are needed but serve different purposes
  - 'Loid''s trust-not-payment axis for ceremony level was a breakthrough: a free unknown agent needs MORE ceremony than a paid verified one, because payment itself is a trust signal'
  - The EULA/OAuth scopes model works for agent permissions when scopes are coarse (5-6 categories) and same-scope updates auto-approve. Fine-grained scopes would recreate the cookie-consent-banner problem
  - 'Existing infrastructure supports more than the team initially assumed: AgentProjectContext.contexts already has per-project data, .paradigm/agents/ directory already supports project-level overrides, buildProfileEnrichment is the natural injection point'
  - Shift's guaranteed-files gap is real — 16+ files that should exist after init are not consistently created, causing downstream tools to fail or return empty results
