body: |-
  ## Session Overview

  Three-round team analysis that evolved from a narrow question (should Documentor maintain university content?) into a foundational agent adoption and permissions system.

  ### Round 1: Documentor University Extension
  - Apex: extend Documentor, two internal phases, 5-op cap
  - Loid: extend, passes "competent coworker" test, but enforce quality bar — notes must add rationale/relationships, not restate .purpose
  - Jinx: KILLED tier mismatch (haiku can't write educational content), proposed flag-only for Documentor + Educator for content creation
  - Judge: approve with conditions (draft-based content, hardcode type:'note' constraint)

  ### Round 2: Agent Project Preferences
  - User proposed: agents should adapt to project preferences on activation, standardize as PAN
  - Apex: full agent-prefs.yaml schema with defaults + per-archetype + marketplace custom sections, PAN spec with 7 requirements
  - Loid: zero required config, progressive disclosure, phase it
  - Jinx: KILLED per-agent preferences — configuration hell, social engineering vector, misdiagnosed problem. Counter-proposed project norms file
  - Synthesis: two features hiding in one proposal (transparency vs configuration)

  ### Round 3: Adoption Ceremony (User's Clarification)
  - User reframed as hiring analogy: one-time adoption, not recurring config. EULA-style scope approval on updates.
  - User: "paradigm shift should always create all core agent files"
  - User: "ship both phases together, go big"
  - Apex: full adoption pipeline spec — adoptions.yaml, shift adoption ceremony, scoped permissions, post-shift recommendations
  - Loid: batch adopt for core (one prompt), individual for marketplace. Trust axis, not payment axis. Smart defaults not blank files.
  - Jinx: BUILD with 3 conditions — coarse scopes, detected-ecosystems-only file creation, consider identity migration

  ## Implementation Scope

  ### New Files
  - `packages/paradigm/src/commands/agent/scopes-types.ts`
  - `packages/paradigm/src/commands/shift-files.ts` (GUARANTEED_FILES manifest)
  - `packages/paradigm/src/commands/shift-recommendations.ts`
  - `packages/paradigm/src/commands/agent/scopes.ts`
  - `packages/paradigm/src/commands/agent/scopes-review.ts`

  ### Modified Files
  - `packages/paradigm/src/commands/shift.ts` — guaranteed files step, adoption ceremony, recommendations engine
  - `packages/paradigm/src/commands/agent/index.ts` — scope handling
  - `packages/paradigm/src/commands/agent/types.ts` — new options types
  - `packages/paradigm/src/index.ts` — register new commands
  - Agent `.agent` files — add `configurable` and `scopes` sections

  ### New CLI Commands
  - `paradigm agent review [id]` — review pending scope changes
  - `paradigm agent approve <id>` — approve current scopes
  - `paradigm agent deny <id>` — deny and revert
  - `paradigm agent scopes <id>` — show approved scopes

  ## PAN v1.0 Requirements (for Neverland)
  1. DECLARE BEHAVIORS — agents must list configurable behaviors with types/defaults
  2. RESPECT PROJECT PREFERENCES — read and apply agent-prefs.yaml
  3. SURFACE CONFIGURATION — support `paradigm agent show <id> --prefs`
  4. FALLBACK GRACEFULLY — missing preferences use declared defaults
  5. NO UNDECLARED SIDE EFFECTS — configurable actions require explicit enablement
  6. REPORT APPLIED PREFERENCES — one-line summary in orchestration output
  7. MARKETPLACE COMPATIBILITY — nevr.land validates declared-behaviors on publish
